INT. GRANDFATHER'S GARAGE - AFTERNOON

Pegboard walls, every wrench outlined in marker. A 1971 pickup under a canvas sheet. A radio on a shelf plays low static.

LEOPOLD VASQUEZ-MBEKI (70s, coveralls, a pencil behind each ear) sits on an upturned bucket, sanding a chair leg. His granddaughter SUNNIVA (9, pigtails, oversized work gloves) holds the leg steady, tongue between her teeth.

On a workbench, a cell phone vibrates against a coffee can.

He glances at it. She glances at him. Neither moves.

                    SUNNIVA
          Is that Dad?

                    LEOPOLD
          Probably.

                    SUNNIVA
          Shouldn't you answer?

                    LEOPOLD
          Probably.

He finishes the stroke. Blows dust off the wood. Hands her the leg. She runs her thumb along the grain, nods, and sets it on the bench.

The phone goes still. A moment later, it buzzes again.

                    LEOPOLD (CONT'D)
          He's persistent.

                    SUNNIVA
          Like you.

                    LEOPOLD
          Like your grandmother.

He wipes his hands on a rag. Reaches for the phone. Taps the screen. Sets it on the bench, on speaker.

                    MURRAY (V.O.)
                (phone)
          Pop? You there?

                    LEOPOLD
          I'm here.

                    MURRAY (V.O.)
                (phone)
          Is Sunni with you?

                    LEOPOLD
          She is. She's sanding.

                    SUNNIVA
          (to the phone)
          I'm not sanding. He's sanding. I'm holding.

                    MURRAY (V.O.)
                (phone)
          Okay. Holding. Can you hold till six? I'm stuck at the office.

                    LEOPOLD
          She can hold till Christmas.

                    SUNNIVA
          I can.

                    MURRAY (V.O.)
                (phone)
          Thanks, Pop. Love you both.

He hangs up before Leopold can reply. Leopold stares at the dead phone. Sunniva picks up the chair leg again and holds it out to him.

                    SUNNIVA
          He didn't say bye.

                    LEOPOLD
          He never does.

He takes the leg. Resumes sanding. She sits on the floor, cross-legged, watching his hands.
